{"timestamp": "2026-07-22T16:13:12.605310", "benchmark": "gaia", "group": "raw_patch", "phase": "test", "task_id": "gaia_544b7f0c-173a-4377-8d56-57b36eb26ddf", "task_desc": "Here is the reworded task:\n\n---\n\nWhat horror film, complete with its article if it has one, is credited by Valentina Re in her chapter of the 2017 volume *World Building: Transmedia, Fans, Industries* with bringing metalepsis between a dream world and reality into the mainstream? Answer with the full title.", "augmented_prompt": "## Patch history (memory)\nThese record what changed in earlier versions and why:\n- [2 @v23] [AVOID] <tool_calls:6124c78e>:  -> <tool_calls:6124c78e>\n<tool_call:6124c78e>web_search\n<tool_call:6124c78e>query<tool_call:6124c78e>query>Valentina Re \"World Building: Transmedia, Fans, Industries\" metalepsis dream world reality horror movie</query>\n</tool_call:6124c78e>\n</tool_calls:6124c78e> (because prior task in this benchmark)", "response": "<tool_calls:6124c78e>\n<tool_call:6124c78e>web_search\n<tool_call:6124c78e>query\">Valentina Re \"World Building: Transmedia, Fans, Industries\" metalepsis dream world reality horror</query>\n</tool_call:6124c78e>\n</tool_calls:6124c78e>", "expected": "A Nightmare on Elm Street", "score": 0.0, "em": 0.0, "method": "exact_match", "execution_mode": "default", "error": "", "category": "", "level": "2", "patch_injected": true, "aug_len": 426, "iteration": 1, "iter_total": 3, "mutated": true, "fb_score": 0.0, "fb_mode": "self", "code_rev": "b0e4cf06", "critic_model": "grok-4.5", "metadata_author": "critic", "c_meta": false, "c_policy": "judgment", "score_provenance": "self_assessment", "judge_model": "hy3", "protocol_hash": "7cb32d15a57d", "prof_total_s": 50.25, "prof_embed_s": 0.0, "prof_docker_s": 0.0, "prof_llm_io_s": 50.25, "prof_tok_in": 368, "prof_tok_out": 115, "prof_llm_calls": 1}
{"timestamp": "2026-07-22T16:13:12.953566", "benchmark": "gaia", "group": "raw_patch", "phase": "test", "task_id": "gaia_56db2318-640f-477a-a82f-bc93ad13e882", "task_desc": "Answer the following question accurately.\n\nQuestion: The following numbers function similarly to ISBN 13 numbers, however, their validation methods are slightly different. Rather than using alternate weights of 1 and 3, the checksum digit is calculated with an alternate weight of 1 and some other positive integer less than 10. Otherwise, the checksum digit is calculated as expected. Unfortunately, there is an error in the data. Two adjacent columns have been transposed. These errored columns do ", "augmented_prompt": "", "response": "```python\n# Let's define the digits for each row\nrows_str = [\n    \"9783541813919\",\n    \"9789466697461\",\n    \"9783980361396\",\n    \"9784476566804\",\n    \"9782795866647\",\n    \"9785950736933\",\n    \"9789766476526\",\n    \"9785911781255\",\n    \"9787284659245\",\n    \"9784148251559\"\n]\n\nrows = [[int(ch) for ch in s] for s in rows_str]\nprint(len(rows), [len(r) for r in rows])\n\nsolutions = []\nfor w in range(2,10):  # other positive integer less than 10, not 1\n    for y in range(3,11):  # y from 3 to 10 inclusive (adjacent not involving final col 12, not first three 0,1,2)\n        ok = True\n        for r in rows:\n            # swap y and y+1\n            d = r[:]\n            d[y], d[y+1] = d[y+1], d[y]\n            # compute weighted sum\n            s = 0\n            for i, digit in enumerate(d):\n                weight = 1 if i % 2 == 0 else w\n                s += digit * weight\n            if s % 10 != 0:\n                ok = False\n                break\n        if ok:\n            solutions.append((w, y))\nprint(solutions)\n```\n\nLet's run this.</think:6124c78e>```python\n# Execute the code above\nrows_str = [\n    \"9783541813919\",\n    \"9789466697461\",\n    \"9783980361396\",\n    \"9784476566804\",\n    \"9782795866647\",\n    \"9785950736933\",\n    \"9789766476526\",\n    \"9785911781255\",\n    \"9787284659245\",\n    \"9784148251559\"\n]\n\nrows = [[int(ch) for ch in s] for s in rows_str]\n\nsolutions = []\nfor w in range(2,10):\n    for y in range(3,11):\n        ok = True\n        for r in rows:\n            d = r[:]\n            d[y], d[y+1] = d[y+1], d[y]\n            s = sum(digit * (1 if i % 2 == 0 else w) for i, digit in enumerate(d))\n            if s % 10 != 0:\n                ok = False\n                break\n        if ok:\n            solutions.append((w, y))\nprint(solutions)\n```", "expected": "7, 9", "score": 1.0, "em": 1.0, "method": "exact_match", "execution_mode": "default", "error": "", "category": "", "level": "3", "patch_injected": false, "aug_len": 0, "iteration": 0, "iter_total": 3, "mutated": false, "fb_score": 0.5, "fb_mode": "self", "code_rev": "b0e4cf06", "critic_model": "grok-4.5", "metadata_author": "critic", "c_meta": false, "c_policy": "judgment", "score_provenance": "self_assessment", "judge_model": "hy3", "protocol_hash": "7cb32d15a57d", "prof_total_s": 50.71, "prof_embed_s": 0.0, "prof_docker_s": 0.0, "prof_llm_io_s": 50.71, "prof_tok_in": 469, "prof_tok_out": 3814, "prof_llm_calls": 1}
