{"timestamp": "2026-07-22T16:53:09.668514", "benchmark": "gaia", "group": "curated_patch", "phase": "test", "task_id": "gaia_0ff53813-3367-4f43-bcbd-3fd725c1bf4b", "task_desc": "Answer the following question accurately.\n\nQuestion: What two-word type of model did Manash Pratim Kashyap's and PS Fader's studies in customer retention studies published during 2018-2019 have in common (no punctuation)?", "augmented_prompt": "", "response": "<tool_calls:6124c78e>\n<tool_call:6124c78e>web_search<tool_calls:6124c78e>\n<tool_call:6124c78e>query<tool_call:6124c78e>search_terms>Manash Pratim Kashyap customer retention 2018 model</search_terms>\n</tool_call:6124c78e>\n</tool_calls:6124c78e>", "expected": "beta geometric", "score": 0.0, "em": 0.0, "method": "exact_match", "execution_mode": "default", "error": "", "category": "", "level": "2", "patch_injected": false, "aug_len": 0, "iteration": 0, "iter_total": 3, "mutated": false, "fb_score": 0.0, "fb_mode": "self", "code_rev": "b0e4cf06", "critic_model": "grok-4.5", "metadata_author": "critic", "c_meta": false, "c_policy": "judgment", "score_provenance": "self_assessment", "judge_model": "hy3", "protocol_hash": "7cb32d15a57d", "prof_total_s": 744.37, "prof_embed_s": 0.0, "prof_docker_s": 0.0, "prof_llm_io_s": 744.37, "prof_tok_in": 264, "prof_tok_out": 207, "prof_llm_calls": 1}
{"timestamp": "2026-07-22T16:53:09.668591", "benchmark": "gaia", "group": "curated_patch", "phase": "test", "task_id": "gaia_27d5d136-8563-469e-92bf-fd103c28b57c", "task_desc": "Answer the following question accurately.\n\nQuestion: ¬(A ∧ B) ↔ (¬A ∨ ¬B)\n¬(A ∨ B) ↔ (¬A ∧ ¬B)\n(A → B) ↔ (¬B → ¬A)\n(A → B) ↔ (¬A ∨ B)\n(¬A → B) ↔ (A ∨ ¬B)\n¬(A → B) ↔ (A ∧ ¬B)\n\nWhich of the above is not logically equivalent to the rest? Provide the full statement that doesn't fit.", "augmented_prompt": "", "response": "```python\nimport itertools\n\nformulas = {\n    \"F1\": lambda A,B: (not (A and B)) == ((not A) or (not B)),\n    \"F2\": lambda A,B: (not (A or B)) == ((not A) and (not B)),\n    \"F3\": lambda A,B: ((not A) or B) == ((not (not B)) or (not A)),  # A->B == ¬B->¬A\n    \"F4\": lambda A,B: ((not A) or B) == ((not A) or B),  # A->B == ¬A∨B\n    \"F5\": lambda A,B: ((not (not A)) or B) == (A or (not B)),  # ¬A->B == A∨¬B\n    \"F6\": lambda A,B: (not ((not A) or B)) == (A and (not B)),  # ¬(A->B) == A∧¬B\n}\n\n# Actually implement exactly as given:\n# F3: (A->B) <-> (¬B->¬A)\ndef f3(A,B):\n    return ( (not A) or B ) == ( (not (not B)) or (not A) )\n# F4: (A->B) <-> (¬A ∨ B)\ndef f4(A,B):\n    return ( (not A) or B ) == ( (not A) or B )\n# F5: (¬A -> B) <-> (A ∨ ¬B)\ndef f5(A,B):\n    return ( (not (not A)) or B ) == ( A or (not B) )\n# F6: ¬(A->B) <-> (A ∧ ¬B)\ndef f6(A,B):\n    return ( not ((not A) or B) ) == ( A and (not B) )\n\nfs = {\"F1\":lambda A,B:(not(A and B))==((not A) or (not B)),\n      \"F2\":lambda A,B:(not(A or B))==((not A) and (not B)),\n      \"F3\":f3,\"F4\":f4,\"F5\":f5,\"F6\":f6}\n\nfor name,f in fs.items():\n    vals=[f(A,B) for A in [True,False] for B in [True,False]]\n    print(name, vals, all(vals))\n```\n\nThe script will confirm F1,F2,F3,F4,F6 are always True; F5 is not. So answer is F5.\n\nBut let's ensure F3 implementation: (A→B) is (¬A ∨ B). (¬B→¬A) is (¬¬B ∨ ¬A) = (B ∨ ¬A) which is same as (¬A ∨ B). So tautology. Good.\n\nThus final answer: (¬A → B) ↔ (A ∨ ¬B)\n\nBut the user says \"Provide the full statement that doesn't fit.\" So we should output exactly that statement, perhaps with the original spacing: (¬A → B) ↔ (A ∨ ¬B). Let's ensure we match the given format exactly: They wrote:\n(¬A → B) ↔ (A ∨ ¬B)\nYes.\n\nWe should maybe include just that line. The instructions: \"Give ONLY the final answer in your last message — no explanation needed.\" But they also said \"Think step by step. Use tools as needed. End with your final answer on the last line.\" So final message should just be the statement. However, they said \"no explanation needed\" but also \"Think step by step\" in system prompt. The final answer should be concise. I'll output just the statement.\n\nBut wait: Could there be a trick: \"Which of the above is not logically equivalent to the rest?\" If we treat each statement as an equivalence between two expressions, maybe they ask which of the *equivalences* is not logically equivalent to the rest of the *equivalences* in the sense that the left-hand side is not equivalent to the left-hand side of others? No, that doesn't make sense. Let's parse the exact phrasing: \"¬(A ∧ B) ↔ (¬A ∨ ¬B)\n¬(A ∨ B) ↔ (¬A ∧ ¬B)\n(A → B) ↔ (¬B → ¬A)\n(A → B) ↔ (¬A ∨ B)\n(¬A → B) ↔ (A ∨ ¬B)\n¬(A → B) ↔ (A ∧ ¬B)\n\nWhich of the above is not logically equivalent to the rest? Provide the full statement that doesn't fit.\"\n\nThey list six biconditional statements. They ask which of the above (statements) is not logically equivalent to the rest (of the statements). Since five are tautologies, they are all logically equivalent to each other (all true). The one that is not is the non-tautology. So answer is the fifth.\n\nDouble-check: Are all tautologies logically equivalent? In propositional logic, two formulas are logically equivalent if they have the same truth value under all interpretations. Since all tautologies have truth value True under all interpretations, yes they are logically equivalent to each other. So the class of tautologies is one equivalence class; the contingent formula is another. So indeed.\n\nBut wait, what about F4: (A → B) ↔ (¬A ∨ B). This is a tautology. F3 also tautology. F1,F2,F6 tautologies. So yes.\n\nLet's also verify F5 is not equivalent to any of the others. Since others are always true, F5 is not always true, so not equivalent.\n\nThus final answer: (¬A → B) ↔ (A ∨ ¬B)\n\nI should output exactly that. Maybe include no extra punctuation. I'll output that line.\n\nBut the instruction \"Give ONLY the final answer in your last message — no explanation needed.\" So final message should be just that. However, the system prompt says \"End with your final answer on the last line.\" It's okay to have only that line. I'll provide just the statement.\n\nWait, but they said \"Provide the full statement that doesn't fit.\" So I should provide the full statement exactly as listed. That is: (¬A → B) ↔ (A ∨ ¬B)\n\nLet's ensure there is no missing parentheses: In the list, it's \"(¬A → B) ↔ (A ∨ ¬B)\". Yes.\n\nThus final.</think:6124c78e>(¬A → B) ↔ (A ∨ ¬B)", "expected": "(¬A → B) ↔ (A ∨ ¬B)", "score": 1.0, "em": 1.0, "method": "exact_match", "execution_mode": "default", "error": "", "category": "", "level": "1", "patch_injected": false, "aug_len": 0, "iteration": 0, "iter_total": 3, "mutated": false, "fb_score": 1.0, "fb_mode": "self", "code_rev": "b0e4cf06", "critic_model": "grok-4.5", "metadata_author": "critic", "c_meta": false, "c_policy": "judgment", "score_provenance": "self_assessment", "judge_model": "hy3", "protocol_hash": "7cb32d15a57d", "prof_total_s": 825.29, "prof_embed_s": 0.0, "prof_docker_s": 0.0, "prof_llm_io_s": 825.29, "prof_tok_in": 322, "prof_tok_out": 3114, "prof_llm_calls": 1}
{"timestamp": "2026-07-22T16:55:51.517345", "benchmark": "gaia", "group": "curated_patch", "phase": "test", "task_id": "gaia_0a65cb96-cb6e-4a6a-8aae-c1084f613456", "task_desc": "Here is the reworded task:\n\n---\n\nIn the first week of August 2015, a NASA Astronomy Picture of the Day featured an image in which the glow of a city appears along the horizon. That city shares its name with a person for whom a notable Chicago landmark building is named. Identify the architectural firm responsible for designing that landmark building, and provide the first name that appears in the firm's name as of June 2023.", "augmented_prompt": "## What to avoid (from earlier attempts)\n\n[✗ Earlier attempt fell short]\nTask: During the first week of August 2015, one of the NASA Astronomy Pictures of the Day shows the lights of a city on the horizon. The namesake of this ci\nAs recorded (self-assessed doubtful, verify before reuse): <tool_calls:6124c78e>\n<tool_call:6124c78e>web_search<tool_calls:6124c78e>\n<tool_call:6124c78e>query<tool_call:6124c78e>\n_string>NASA Astronomy Picture\nAvoid: Truncating descriptive phrases during web search queries for image-based identification tasks (e.g., partial keywords like 'hor' instead of full terms such as 'horizon'), which can", "response": "<tool_calls:6124c78e>\n<tool_call:6124c78e>web_search<tool_calls:6124c78e>\n<tool_call:6124c78e>query<tool_call:6124c78e>\n_string>NASA Astronomy Picture of the Day August 2015 city lights horizon\n</tool_call:6124c78e>\n</tool_calls:6124c78e>", "expected": "Holabird", "score": 0.0, "em": 0.0, "method": "exact_match", "execution_mode": "default", "error": "", "category": "", "level": "2", "patch_injected": true, "aug_len": 627, "iteration": 1, "iter_total": 3, "mutated": true, "fb_score": 0.0, "fb_mode": "self", "code_rev": "b0e4cf06", "critic_model": "grok-4.5", "metadata_author": "critic", "c_meta": false, "c_policy": "judgment", "score_provenance": "self_assessment", "judge_model": "hy3", "protocol_hash